        {"h2_html": "The inquiry you miss is the client who <em>hires the trainer who answered</em>",
         "body_html": '<p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch">A trainer counting reps on the floor, spotting a heavy lift, or walking a client through a movement cannot stop to grab a ringing phone or a message, and a prospect who has just decided to get serious is not going to leave a voicemail and wait around. They fire off a message to two or three trainers at once and hire whoever replies first. The work in front of you, the very thing they want to pay you for, is the reason the phone rings out, and that prospect walks straight to the trainer who happened to be free to answer. A new personal-training client is not a single appointment you can shrug off when you miss it. Signing one almost always means a block of sessions or a coaching month that pays out over weeks, so a missed inquiry is not a lost hour, it is a lost client and everything they would have spent while they trained with you.</p><p style="color:var(--ink-2);line-height:1.85;max-width:64ch;margin-top:1rem">An answering service picks up the moment the message lands, day or night, sounds encouraging instead of harried, asks what the person is after and where they are starting from, and either books a free consult on the spot or takes the details so the lead never disappears. The prospect ends up captured and scheduled with you rather than handed to the trainer down the road who simply got to the phone first, and you never had to break away from the client you were already being paid to coach.</p>'},
